## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design efficient streaming algorithms that trade precision for memory and speed when computing quantiles—a fundamental requirement in real-time monitoring systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantile estimation using fixed-bin histograms
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs in one-pass algorithms
- Update complexity O(1) per element, query complexity O(K)
- Error analysis and parameter selection (K vs accuracy)
- Comparison with exact solutions using balanced search trees

## Code Example
```javascript
class FixedBinQuantile {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M;
    this.K = K;
    this.binWidth = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(M / K));
    this.bins = new Array(K).fill(0);
    this.total = 0;
  }

  add(x) {
    const idx = Math.min(this.K - 1, Math.floor(x / this.binWidth));
    this.bins[idx]++;
    this.total++;
  }

  getPercentile(p) {
    const target = Math.ceil(p * this.total);
    let cumulative = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.K; i++) {
      cumulative += this.bins[i];
      if (cumulative >= target) {
        return (i + 1) * this.binWidth; // Upper bound of bin
      }
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding how to count inversions online with limited memory and batched updates tests both data structures and amortized reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree for prefix sums; online per-element updates
- Inversion counting formula: add current_seen - count_of_≤v
- Amortized batch analysis and memory scaling with M

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.f=[0]; for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) this.f[i]=0; }
  add(i,delta){ for(i+=1;i<this.f.length;i+=i&-i) this.f[i]+=delta }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(i+=1;i>0;i-=i&-i) s+=this.f[i]; return s }
}
function processBatch(batch, M, fen, totalSeen){ let inversions=0; for(const v of batch){ inversions += totalSeen - fen.sum(v); fen.add(v,1); totalSeen++; } return {inversions, totalSeen}; }
```

